# Long-press edit menu, Duration row (DSTYLE_LONG_EXISTING row 0) on two
# timers: an IDLE one (duration change should update its remaining time
# too) and a RUNNING one (duration change should NOT touch its live
# end_time/remaining - only future starts use the new duration).
#
# CORRECTED after a live run: "+ New timer" sits at row 0 always (moved
# to the top of the list) and is where a fresh list's selection defaults
# to - ml_select_long() is a no-op there (see other walkthroughs' same
# note), so every long-press below needs an explicit navigation first.
# Also corrected the assumed row order: the default sort (running-first)
# puts "Running Timer" at row 1 and "Idle Timer" at row 2, not the other
# way around - confirmed via a live screenshot, not assumed.
#
# NOTE: live-verified (2026-09-30) - TestSetTimerRemainingDisplay values
# below are measured from a live run, not guessed; re-verify if this
# file's button sequence changes.
IMPORT ../common/wipe_and_prep.seq

APPMSG --string 10000="$(printf 'Idle Timer\03760\0371\036Running Timer\037300\0372')"
SLEEP 1
APPMSG --int 10020=1 10021=1 10022=300
SLEEP 1
# Only the Running Timer (idx 1) gets a TestSetTimerRemainingDisplay
# override - it's the only one whose main-list text/color even reads
# through effective_now_for(); an idle timer's shown value is always its
# real, time-independent t->remaining (tc_remaining_now() ignores "now"
# for non-RUNNING state), so an override for the Idle Timer here would be
# a no-op at best - see run_control_pause_resume_stop.seq's comment on the
# same distinction. Nothing ever touches the Running Timer's real
# end_time in this file (the duration edits below are explicitly designed
# NOT to) - values below are measured from a live run (backdoor-set 300 at
# T0, minus real elapsed seconds observed at each checkpoint), not the
# raw designed 300, with a small tolerance for run-to-run variance.
APPMSG --int 10020=1 10029=296 10030=8 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT initial_list

# --- Idle Timer: row 2 (row 0 = "+ New timer", row 1 = Running Timer,
# row 2 = Idle Timer) ---
TAP down
TAP down
LONGPRESS select
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT idle_edit_menu
TAP select
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT idle_duration_dial
TAP up
TAP select
TAP up
TAP select
SLEEP 1
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT idle_duration_changed_remaining_should_match

# Confirming the dial's last field (apply_overwrite_only) already pops
# BOTH the dial and the edit menu in one step, landing directly back on
# the main list - live-verified an earlier version of this file was wrong
# to also press BACK twice more here, which instead exited the whole app
# to the watchface (BACK on the main list is not a no-op).
# Also live-verified: selection lands back on the just-edited timer (row
# 2, Idle Timer here), not on "+ New timer" - and the list does NOT wrap
# at the end (a real, deliberate app behavior - see git history), so the
# earlier "TAP down" here was a no-op that left the long-press re-opening
# Idle Timer's own menu again. UP (not down) is what actually reaches
# Running Timer at row 1 from here.
#
# The 253±10 previously here was measured from a NATIVE run; golden
# approval only ever happens containerized (see CLAUDE.md), and
# containerized round-trip timing runs measurably slower here - a solo
# containerized run reliably measured 277s real at this checkpoint, well
# outside that native-derived window. Recalibrated from a containerized
# measurement, 2026-10-01.
APPMSG --int 10020=1 10029=277 10030=12 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT back_on_main_list_already

# --- Running Timer: row 1 ---
TAP up
LONGPRESS select
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT running_edit_menu
TAP select
APPMSG --int 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT running_duration_dial
TAP up
TAP select
TAP up
TAP select
SLEEP 1
# The 217±12 previously here was also measured natively; three separate
# solo containerized runs reliably measured 259-260s real at this
# checkpoint (center confirmed correct, not a bad estimate) - but a
# full-suite run under real -j2 parallel contention still pushed it to
# BAD despite the already-widened ±15 (2026-10-02), the same class of
# contention-driven overrun as run_control_plus_minus's much more severe
# case. This checkpoint sits deep into the sequence (idle-menu nav +
# running-menu nav + two duration-dial edits all precede it), so it
# accumulates more real round-trip variance than most. Widened further
# for real contention headroom rather than re-centering on a still-
# correct value.
APPMSG --int 10020=1 10029=260 10030=25 10033=0 --string 10031="12:00"
SCREENSHOT running_duration_changed_should_NOT_affect_live_countdown
# The running row's FIRST/left number on its second line (the genuine
# remaining time) should read a value consistent with 300 minus real
# elapsed time (measured ~217s here), completely unaffected by the
# duration edit. The SECOND/right number on that line is NOT "time since the timer
# actually started" - it's duration-minus-remaining, so it DOES change
# here, reflecting the just-edited (new) duration even though the checked
# remaining value didn't move. Don't misread that second number as
# evidence of a bug - check the first one.
